.TH POCKETDESK 1 "2026-09-24" "Pocketdesk 0.2.0"
.SH NAME
pocketdesk \- Remote desktop over Tailscale with KDE Connect integration
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pocketdesk
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fICOMMAND\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Pocketdesk (formerly Linux Link) is a pure-Rust remote desktop solution for Linux and Windows.
It combines KDE Connect device integration with low-latency
screen streaming over Tailscale.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B start
Start the server daemon
.TP
.B stop
Stop a running daemon started with
.B "pocketdesk start"
.TP
.B status
Print local tailscale status
.TP
.B sessions [\-\-count \fIN\fR] [\-\-json]
Show recorded streaming\-session outcomes (default: last 10); with
.BR \-\-json ,
emit the retained per\-session records as JSON lines (one object per session,
including the p50/p90/p95/p99/max tails) instead of the human tally.
The tails name who measured them: \fIrtt_*\fR and \fIenc_*\fR are the desktop's
own readings, \fIdec_*\fR, \fIrnd_*\fR and \fIe2e_*\fR are reported back by the
device doing the decoding, and \fIphone_rtt\fR/\fIphone_lost\fR are that
device's view of the link. \fIenc_*\fR appears as soon as frames are encoded,
but \fIrtt_*\fR, the link block and the device's \fIphone_*\fR reading all hang
off the server's five\-second sampling period, so a connect\-and\-drop shorter
than that reports none of them.
Each record also carries the transport's own account of the link: packets,
bytes and datagrams lost or sent, how many times the selected path changed
address, and how many seconds it rode a relay. QUIC (LAN) connections add the
peak congestion window, the congestion\-event count, the path MTU the library
discovered and any black hole it detected; iroh WAN connections omit those
four, because its connection statistics aggregate them away rather than
reporting zero.
.TP
.B bench [\-\-target software|vaapi] [\-\-frames \fIN\fR] [\-\-repeat \fIN\fR] [\-\-baseline \fIPATH\fR] [\-\-record \fIPATH\fR] [\-\-tolerance-pct \fIPCT\fR] [\-\-json]
Run the pinned encoder benchmark on this machine: the same generated 720p clip,
the same bitrate and preset every time, reported as per\-frame encode percentiles
(p50/p90/p95/p99/max). With
.BR \-\-baseline ,
grade the run against a committed record \(em a file, or a directory of records
matched by host and backend \(em and exit
.B 1
if a percentile grew beyond
.BR \-\-tolerance-pct .
Exit
.B 3
means no baseline matches this machine, which is reported as a skip rather than
a pass: encode throughput does not travel between CPUs.
.B \-\-record
writes the measured record out, naming it after backend + host when given a
directory. See
.I bench/README.md
in the source tree.
.TP
.B list
List peers currently visible on the tailnet
.TP
.B watch [\-\-interval \fISECONDS\fR]
Continuously watch peer discovery events (default: 10s)
.TP
.B capabilities [\-\-json | \-\-markdown]
Show the negotiated protocol versions, transports, capture backends and codecs
this build actually speaks \(em generated from the constants the wire uses \(em
followed by the configured KDE Connect capability sets and trusted devices.
.B \-\-json
emits the machine\(hyreadable report and
.B \-\-markdown
emits the source of
.IR docs/capabilities.md ;
both are mutually exclusive.
.TP
.B connect \fIPEER\fR [\-\-port \fIPORT\fR]
Connect to a peer and perform a basic control\-channel handshake
.TP
.B pair [\fIPIN\fR] [\-\-grant \fIDURATION\fR]
Set or generate a temporary 6\-digit pairing PIN;
.B \-\-grant
(s/m/h/d, e.g. 15m, 1h30m) puts a time limit on the trust the PIN grants, for
one\-off support sessions
.TP
.B unpair [\fIDEVICE\-ID\fR]
Remove a paired device from the trust store (all devices if omitted)
.TP
.B kick \fITARGET\fR
Drop a live streaming session: device id, a unique id prefix of >=6 chars,
the peer IP, or
.B all
.TP
.B relay [\-\-port \fIPORT\fR] [\-\-hostname \fINAME\fR [\-\-email \fIADDR\fR]] [\-\-allow \fIID\fR]... [\-\-allow\-paired] [\-\-open]
Run a relay for phones away from home, in place of n0's public one.
Plain HTTP on
.I PORT
(default 3340), or with
.B \-\-hostname
HTTPS on ports 80 and 443 with a Let's Encrypt certificate.
It admits only the endpoints listed with
.B \-\-allow
(repeatable) and, with
.BR \-\-allow\-paired ,
this desktop and the phones paired with it now (by the WAN key each
registered on a connection at home);
.B \-\-open
admits anyone. With none of these it refuses to start.
Point the desktop at it with
.B relay_urls
in config.toml.
.TP
.B relay\-ids
Print this desktop's WAN id and each paired phone's, for
.B \-\-allow
on a relay that runs elsewhere
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-verbose
Verbose output
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Print help
.sp
There is no
.B \-\-config
or top\(hylevel
.B \-\-port
flag: the server reads its configuration from the path below, and per\-command
flags are listed with each command above.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/pocketdesk/config.toml
Configuration file.
.B dht_publish
says what the desktop publishes on the Mainline DHT under its WAN id, so a
phone away from home finds it again:
.B relay
(the relay URLs, the default),
.B addresses
(also its public IP addresses) or
.BR off .
Private, LAN and tailnet addresses are never published
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/pocketdesk/streaming_sessions.log
Session outcome log, tab\-separated \fIkey=value\fR lines, rotated at 1 MiB
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/pocketdesk/streaming_sessions.jsonl
The same reports as one JSON object per session \- the record a benchmark
baseline is compared against \- rotated at 4 MiB
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/pocketdesk/certs/
Streaming TLS/QUIC identity (private key); never copy this into a repository
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/pocketdesk/relay\-acme/
The relay's Let's Encrypt account and certificates (HTTPS mode)
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUST_LOG
Log level (trace, debug, info, warn, error)
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tailscaled (8),
.BR hyprland (1)
